    {
      "name": "shared-slots-three-authors-seen-from-the-lowest-uuid",
      "bug": null,
      "why": "Each author numbers their own rows densely from zero, so authors who move a row at the same moment land different ids on the same sortOrder. The id settles it — and it has to settle it identically on every device, so this vector and the two that follow merge the SAME three slices from each seat in turn and expect byte-for-byte the same order. Two slots are shared here, three rows each, and the slices are handed over so that BOTH slots arrive in the exact REVERSE of the order expected back: an implementation that leaves the losers of a slot collision wherever its per-id map happened to iterate returns this list backwards, twice. The arrangement is the vector rather than decoration. An earlier version put two rows on one slot, and a no-tiebreak implementation passed it anyway (lc-3of) — two rows have exactly one wrong permutation, so a per-id map that iterates in hash order rather than insertion order can land on the right one by luck. Three rows on each of two slots leaves 35 of 36 orderings wrong.",
